# Copy this file to .env.local and fill it in. .env.local is gitignored — never commit it.
#
#   cp web/.env.example web/.env.local
#
# Nothing in .env.local is sent to the browser. It is read by the server only.

# --- Required -------------------------------------------------------------------

# Your rotated Gemini key, from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
#
# Also used for the semantic half of the in-page find (`find_in_page`), which embeds a page's
# passages to match them by meaning. No extra key or configuration: if the embedding call
# fails or no key is set, the find falls back to fuzzy lexical matching alone and says so in
# its result — the ranking degrades, the answer does not.
# Restrict it while you're there: scope it to the Generative Language API and set a
# quota cap. A leaked capped key costs money once; a leaked uncapped one keeps costing.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# --- Strongly recommended before this is reachable by anyone else -----------------

# Shared passphrase. Leave blank for localhost-only use. Set it before you deploy —
# without it, anyone who finds the URL can spend your quota.
APP_PASSWORD=

# --- Web search: where the citations come from ------------------------------------
#
# The assistant is not allowed to assert a fact it did not retrieve, so search is the only
# way it learns anything. With no key set it falls back to scraping DuckDuckGo's HTML
# endpoint, which needs no account and is rate-limited without warning — fine for trying
# the app, not fine for anything anyone relies on. Set one of these before that matters.
#
# Whichever key is present is used, in this order: Brave, Tavily, Serper, Google.
#
# Names below are the canonical spelling, but casing doesn't matter — TAVILY_API_KEY,
# Tavily_API_key and tavily_api_key are all found. A key the app can't see doesn't error,
# it silently falls back to DuckDuckGo, which then gets blocked; `npm run dev` prints
# which provider it settled on, so check that line if searches start failing.

# https://brave.com/search/api/ — free tier, no card.
BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY=

# https://tavily.com — built for this use; returns cleaned page text with the results.
# Note: asking for `freshness` on a Tavily search also switches it to the news topic,
# because Tavily ignores the date window on any other topic.
TAVILY_API_KEY=

# https://serper.dev — Google results through an API.
SERPER_API_KEY=

# https://developers.google.com/custom-search/v1/overview — needs both halves: the API
# key and the search engine ID of a Programmable Search Engine set to search the whole web.
GOOGLE_CSE_KEY=
GOOGLE_CSE_CX=

# Force one provider when several keys are set. brave | tavily | serper | google | duckduckgo
# SEARCH_PROVIDER=

# Set to false to turn search off entirely. The citation audit goes with it — the
# assistant then answers from its training data, uncited and unchecked, which is exactly
# the behaviour this app exists to avoid. For debugging only.
# WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED=true

# --- Optional -------------------------------------------------------------------

# Spend caps, per client. Defaults shown.
RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=15
RATE_LIMIT_PER_DAY=300

# Longest single message accepted, in characters.
MAX_INPUT_CHARS=8000

# How many past turns get resent as context. Higher = better memory, more tokens.
MAX_TURNS=20

# Longest single reply Gemini may generate, in tokens. Output tokens cost more than
# input ones, and unlike everything else above, nothing capped this before.
#
# Note this covers the model's *thinking* as well as its answer, and the models at the head
# of the chain think before they search and again before they write. A number chosen as
# "more prose than any fact-check needs" is therefore not what the answer gets — set it too
# low and the reply stops mid-sentence, right where the citation goes. THINKING_BUDGET_TOKENS
# below is what actually reserves room for the visible answer; raising this alone just
# raises the pool the reasoning draws from too.
MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=16384

# How many of those tokens the model may spend on its internal reasoning, via Gemini's
# thinkingConfig.thinkingBudget. Reasoning and the visible answer draw from the same
# MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS pool unless this caps the reasoning specifically — this is the actual
# fix for an answer truncated mid-verdict, not a bigger MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS on its own.
# Sent only to models believed to support it (the "3" series and 2.5, not 2.0-flash); an
# unrecognised field is treated as a reason to try the next model rather than fail the turn.
# Set to 0 to turn the field off entirely rather than requesting a zero budget.
THINKING_BUDGET_TOKENS=4096

# The same cap, for a round that still holds its search tools.
#
# A turn runs up to three model calls, and the early ones are not writing the answer —
# they are deciding what to look up. Handing those the full reasoning budget is the largest
# avoidable wait in a video fact-check: the reader sits through thousands of tokens of
# invisible deliberation before the first search is even dispatched, then again next round.
# Enumerating a clip's claims is a shallower task than weighing what came back, so it is
# budgeted like one; the round that writes the verdict keeps THINKING_BUDGET_TOKENS above.
#
# Lower this to make the app answer sooner and think less about what to search for. Set it
# to 0 to go back to one budget for every round. It never *raises* THINKING_BUDGET_TOKENS —
# the smaller of the two is what gets sent.
TOOL_ROUND_THINKING_BUDGET_TOKENS=1024

# Milliseconds to hold the final answer back before showing any of it, so a mid-stream
# failure can fail over to the next model in the chain silently instead of leaving a
# broken turn on screen. 0 (the default) sends every token immediately, exactly as before.
#
# The problem this solves: once a token of the answer has reached the reader, that round
# can't swap models anymore — the reader has already seen the start of an answer no other
# model can continue. Gemini ends a turn on RECITATION or SAFETY with no warning, and this
# app makes that more likely than an ordinary chat: `find_in_page` hands the model verbatim
# page passages to quote, and quoting closely is what a recitation filter looks for.
#
# Turned on, the answer is buffered for this many milliseconds after its first token. A
# failure inside that window is invisible — the buffer is dropped, the next model in the
# chain is tried, and the reader never knows the first one failed. A failure after the
# window is exactly as unrecoverable as it is today; this only widens the window, not
# removes the seam. Only the answering round is held — the earlier tool-calling rounds are
# unaffected, so this adds no wait before the first search.
#
# The cost is exactly this many milliseconds added to the wait before the answer starts,
# on every turn, whether or not that turn was ever going to fail — which is why it defaults
# to off rather than being turned on for everyone. 800-1500 is a reasonable range to try:
# short enough that a clean turn barely notices, long enough to catch a stop that happens
# near the start of the answer, which is where recitation stops land.
# ANSWER_HOLD_MS=0

# How much of each video frame Gemini looks at: low | medium | high. Unset by default.
#
# The biggest remaining speed lever for short-form video, and the one with a real cost. A
# clip is sampled at a frame a second, so a 45-second TikTok reaches the model as ~45
# images — several thousand input tokens it must read before saying anything, on every
# round of the turn. `low` cuts that by roughly four.
#
# What it buys the time back from is detail in the frame, which on short-form video is
# often where the claim actually is: a text card on screen, not a sentence in the audio.
# So this is off unless you turn it on. If you do, try `medium` first and check it against
# clips whose claim is written rather than spoken before going to `low`.
# GEMINI_MEDIA_RESOLUTION=

# Override the model fallback chain (comma-separated, tried in order).
# Default: gemini-3.6-flash,gemini-3.5-flash,gemini-3-flash-preview,gemini-2.5-flash,gemini-2.0-flash
# GEMINI_MODEL_CHAIN=

# System prompt for the assistant. The default is the fact-checking prompt in
# web/lib/verified-chat.js: it tells the model its facts come from the web_search tool and
# that every claim needs a citation. Overriding it replaces the *instruction* only — the
# citation audit still runs, and an answer that cites nothing is still sent back for a
# rewrite. Override with care; a prompt that doesn't mention the rule makes the model fail
# the audit and answer slowly rather than making it answer freely.
# SYSTEM_PROMPT=You are a helpful assistant.

# How many bytes of downloaded clip may be held for the next turn. Default 48 MB.
#
# Gemini has no memory between requests, so every turn replays the whole conversation and
# re-attaches each clip at its first mention — the bytes have to be in the request body or
# the model can't see the video it is being asked about. Without a cache that means a
# ten-turn thread about one TikTok downloads the same MP4 ten times, and every follow-up
# question waits out the full resolve-and-download before a token can appear.
#
# A clip is kept for ten minutes; the oldest are dropped to stay under this ceiling. It is
# process memory on a warm instance, and a download already costs its own size plus a
# base64 copy while a request is in flight, so this is deliberately about one maximum-sized
# clip's worth — which is roughly ten typical ones, short-form video being a few MB each.
# Set to 0 to turn the cache off and fetch every clip fresh on every turn.
# CLIP_CACHE_MAX_BYTES=50331648

# Instagram's persisted-query id, if the built-in one stops working.
#
# Reels reach the model as bytes, and finding those bytes means running the same query
# instagram.com's own web client runs: POST /graphql/query with a `doc_id` and a shortcode.
# That id is a server-side query hash Instagram rotates on its own schedule — not an API,
# and nothing promises the built-in default outlives any particular week.
#
# When it rotates, the query starts answering `{"errors":[…],"data":null}` and every
# Instagram link reports that the doc_id may need rotating. Read the current one out of a
# logged-out instagram.com session's network panel (the request whose x-fb-friendly-name is
# PolarisPostActionLoadPostQueryQuery) and set it here. Comma-separated ids are tried in
# order, so a replacement can be rolled out before the old one is retired.
# INSTAGRAM_DOC_ID=
